    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d462">

                <pb facs="1101" n="1067" sameAs="frus:frus1947v02/pg1067"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Germany)/9–147: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Political Adviser for Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Murphy</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-09-01T15:00:00+02:00">September 1,
                            1947—3 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3006. As anticipated, 69th meeting Control Council<anchor n="26" xml:id="d462-f1"
                    /> held August 30 brought forth lengthy Soviet protest against new
                    British-American bizonal industry level announcement. Referring to governmental
                    note August 18,<anchor n="27" xml:id="d462-f2"/> latest bizonal measures branded
                    as rupture of Potsdam Agreement and quadripartite procedure, and another step in
                    process of dismemberment of Germany, following original bizonal merger and
                    subsequent establishment Economic Council. German resources will thus be taken
                    away from German hands and come under influence certain foreign monopoly
                    interests.</p>
                <pb facs="1102" n="1068" xml:id="pg1068"/>
                <p>French member<anchor n="28" xml:id="d462-f3"/> referred only to official French
                    communiqué published day before. British member<anchor n="29" xml:id="d462-f4"/>
                    quoted in his reply from official Foreign Office reply to Soviet Government. US
                        member,<anchor n="30" xml:id="d462-f5"/> while reserving right reply later
                    in detail, emphasized great efforts made by US delegation for 2 years to achieve
                    economic unification Germany, that invitation to other powers to join present
                    bi-zonal merger still open, but meanwhile US not prepared sit by and watch
                    American zone become economic quagmire unable support self or contribute
                    European recovery.</p>
                <p>Repeated London as 316; Paris as 386.</p>
                <p>
                    Dept please relay Moscow as 476.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Murphy</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d462-f1" n="26" xml:id="d462-f1-content">
                <p>I.e., Allied Control Council for Germany; for additional documentation on the
                    participation of the United States in the work of the Allied Control Authority
                    for Germany, see <ref target="#pg831">pp. 831</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d462-f2" n="27" xml:id="d462-f2-content">
                <p>Regarding the note of August 18, 1947, from Soviet Chargé Tsarapkin to the Acting Secretary of State, see
                    the editorial note, <ref target="#pg1039">p. 1039</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d462-f3" n="28" xml:id="d462-f3-content">
                <p>General Koenig.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d462-f4" n="29" xml:id="d462-f4-content">
                <p>Marshal Douglas.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d462-f5" n="30" xml:id="d462-f5-content">
                <p>General Clay.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
